      "writtenOn": "2023-01-31T21:51:18Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I discussed offline with Nicolette about this approach and will try and summarize our thoughts here vs. inline with the code.\n\n1. In general, having to introduce this extra copy and extra resource seems like it\u0027s going to have a large negative performance impact. These issues around loading and resolving to the same texture are likely to impact many adopters of webgpu that would want to do memoryless MSAA attachments. I strongly think we should figure out how to have Dawn support this. In particular, Nicolette\u0027s review of the GPU backends does not suggest that hardware prevents us from doing this. In particular, https://github.com/gpuweb/gpuweb/issues/108 that Nicolette found does not seem like it concluded with an inability to load and resolve on any of the backends that Dawn needs to support.\n   - Is this just not yet implemented in Dawn?\n   - Does webgpu need to be further updated to refine this part of the spec?\n   - Are there ways we can configure a subpass to do this, along the lines we would do for Vulkan?\n   - Some of the CLs referenced in this bug https://bugs.chromium.org/p/dawn/issues/detail?id\u003d56 make it sound like it should be possible in Dawn? We may just need to set up the descriptors differently?\n   \n2. Assuming #1 cannot be resolved quickly (which is unlikely), I am wondering if a slightly lighter weight approach would be to not use memoryless/discardable MSAA attachments in Dawn. Then when the Dawn command buffer processes a graphite renderpass, it loads from the \"resolve\" texture and draws to the msaa texture with a store action. Then when that encoder is finished, it adds a blit render encoder to manually resolve the msaa texture to the \"resolve\" texture. Because they are in split into two encoders, we should avoid the validation error of sampling and resolving to the same texture.\n\n3. Even if #2 is not possible, or too expensive relative to extra texture+copy, I think this leaks too much into the high-level graphite API and I\u0027m not sure it\u0027s necessary. The DawnCaps can expose its own functions for determining a msaaLoadtextureinfo that any Dawn component can access. The command buffer or queue manager can still use the DawnResourceProvider to get a load texture if it needs it (assuming #2 doesn\u0027t work out). The behavior would remain hidden from the outer logic.",
      "revId": "d5318f329ef97c2a431bfc629ffbf3a03bb55cab",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
